wandlōn'' = D. ''wandelen'' = OHG. ''wantalōn'', MHG. G. ''wandern'', ''wandeln'' = Sw. ''vandra'' = Dan. ''vandre'', wander, travel, walk; a freq. form, associated with ''wend'' (AS. ''wendan'', etc.), < AS. ''windan'' (pret. ''wand''), wind, turn, twist: see ''wind''<sup>1</sup>, ''wend''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To ramble without, or as if without, any certain course or object in view; travel or move from place to place; range about; roam; rove; stroll; stray.
{{smaller block|He ''wandereth'' abroad for bread. {{right|Job xv. 23.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wandering'', each his several way
Pursues, as inclination or sad choice
Leads him perplexed.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 523.}}}}
'''2.''' To leave home or a settled place of abode; depart; migrate.
{{smaller block|When God caused me to ''wander'' from my father’s house. {{right|Gen. xx. 13.}}}}
'''3.''' To depart from any settled course; go astray, as from the paths of duty; stray; deviate; err.
{{smaller block|You ''wander'' from the good we aim at. {{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., iii. 1. 138.}}}}
'''4.''' To lose one’s way; be lost. [Colloq.]—'''5.''' To think or speak incoherently; rave; be delirious.
{{smaller block|<poem>Litill he sleppit,
But ''wandrit'' & woke for woo of his buernes.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 10097.}}}}
{{smaller block|Tom Bendibow seemed to have something on his mind, but I think he ''wanders'' a little. He may speak more explicitly to you. {{right|''J. Hawthorne'', Dust, p. 222.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 1-3.''' ''Roam'', ''Rove'', etc. (see ''ramble''), straggle.—'''3.''' Swerve, digress.}}

