¶
'''wandering''' (won′dėr-ing), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wanderynge'', ''wandringe'' (= MHG. ''wanderinge'', G. ''wanderung''), verbal n. of ''wander'', ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of one who wanders; a ramble or peregrination; a journeying hither and thither.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>And many a tree and bush my ''wanderings'' know,
And e’en the clouds and silent stars of heaven.</poem>
{{right|''Jones Very'', Poems, p. 85.}}}}

'''2.''' A straying away, as from one’s home or the right way; a deviation or digression in any way or from any course: as, the ''wandering'' of the thoughts; a ''wandering'' from duty.
{{smaller block|Let him now recover his ''wanderings.''
{{right|''Decay of Christian Piety.''}}}}

'''3.''' Incoherence of speech; raving; delirium.

'''wanderingly''' (won′dėr-ing-li), ''adv.'' In a wandering or unsteady manner.
{{smaller block|When was Lancelot ''wanderingly'' lewd?
{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}

'''wandering-sailor''' (won′dėr-ing-sā″lọr), ''n.'' The moneywort, ''Lysimachia Nummularia'', and the Kenilworth ivy or wandering Jew, ''Linaria Cymbalaria'', from their creeping habit.

'''wanderment†''' (won′dėr-ment), ''n.'' [< ''wander'' + ''-ment.''] The act of roaming or roving. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Barefoot went
Upon their ten toes in wild ''wanderment.''</poem>
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, II. iii. 20.}}}}

'''wanderoo''' (won-de-rö′), ''n.'' [Also ''wanderow'', ''wanderu''; = F. ''ouanderou'' (Buffon), < Cingalese ''wanderu'', a monkey; cf. Hind. ''bandar'', a monkey: see ''bunder.''] A large catarrhine monkey of Malabar, India, ''Macacus silenus.'' {{smaller|It is about 3 feet long to the tip of the tail (which is tufted), of a blackish color with pink buttocks, and has an extravagant mane of long hair surrounding the face, of a light or whitish}}

