'''wandle''' (won′dl), ''a.'' [Appar. for *''wandly'', < ''wand'' + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''wandy''.] Wand-like; wandy; supple; pliant; nimble. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''wandoo''' (won′dö), ''n.'' [Native Australian.] A eucalypt, ''Eucalyptus redunca'', the white-gum of western Australia. {{smaller|It is a large tree, the trunk sometimes 17 feet in diameter, in one variety suddenly swelling out near the ground. It furnishes a very pale heavy, hard, tough, and durable wood, greatly prized for wheelwork, especially for fellies.}}

'''wandreth†''' (won′dreth), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wandreth, wandrethe, wondrethe'', < Icel. ''vandrædhi'', difficulty, trouble, genit. as adj., difficult, troublesome, < ''vandr'', difficult, requiring pains and care, hence also select, choice, picked, also zealous, + ''rādh'', advice, counsel, management, = E. ''read:'' see ''read''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', and cf. ''-reth'', ''-red'', in ''hundreth, hundred, kindred.'' Cf. ''quandary''.] Difficulty; peril; distress.
{{smaller block|<poem>Bettur is a buerne by hym sum pes
Than in ''wandreth'' & woo to wepe all his lyue.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 11514.}}}}

'''wands''' (wondz), ''n. pl.'' [Prob. < Dan. ''vand'', water, = Norw. ''vand'', water, a lake, tarn: see ''water''.] Roads; a roadstead.
{{smaller block|The 21 day the Primerose remaining at an anker in the ''wands'', the other three shippes bare into Orwel hauen.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 310.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
wonia = OHG. ''wanōn'', ''wanen'' = Icel. ''vana'', decrease, wane; from the adj., AS. ''wan'' = OHG. ''wan'' = Icel. ''vanr'' = Goth. ''wans'', wanting, deficient (an adj. also appearing as a negative prefix: see ''wan-''), = Skt. ''ūna'', lacking, deficient, inferior; perhaps an orig. pp. of a root ''u'', be empty, Zend √ ''u'', be lacking, existing also in Gr. εὖνις, bereaved, G. ''öde'', desolate, etc. Cf. ''wan''<sup>1</sup>, ''want''<sup>1</sup>. Hence prob. ''waniand'', ''wanion''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To decrease; be diminished: applied particularly to the periodical lessening of the illuminated part of the moon: opposed to ''wax.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Undernethe hir feet she hadde a mone,
Wexing it was, and sholde ''wanie'' sone.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight’s Tale, l. 1220.}}
<poem>How slow
This old moon ''wanes!''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., i. 1. 4.}}}}
'''2.''' To decline; fail; sink; approach an end.
{{smaller block|Wealth and ease in ''waning'' age.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 142.}}
Daylight ''waned'', and night came on.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Balder Dead.}}}}
'''II.†''' ''trans.'' To cause to decrease; lessen.
{{smaller block|That he [Christ] takes the name of the son of a woman, and ''wanes'' the glorious name of the Son of God.
{{right|''Donne'', Sermons, iii.}}}}

'''wane'''<sup>1</sup> (wān), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wane'', < AS. ''wana'' = Icel. ''vani'', decrease, wane: see ''wane''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' Periodic decrease of the illuminated part of the moon; period of decreasing illumination.
{{smaller block|<poem>How many a time hath Phœbe from her ''wane''
With Phœbus’ fires filled up her horns again.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', On his Lady’s not Coming to London.}}}}
